---
/**
 * /privacy — the trust anchor.
 *
 * Every claim on this page was checked against the code before it was written,
 * and the page says so about the one exception rather than rounding down to
 * "no third parties": `faviconUrl()` in `lib/tools.ts` is rendered by
 * `components/ToolList.astro` and `pages/tools/[slug].astro`, so the tool pages
 * really do hit Google. A privacy page that is wrong about its own build is
 * worth less than no page at all.
 *
 * Not in the nav or the section manifest on purpose: it is a footer page.
 */
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
---

<Base
  title="Privacy · Aayush Manchanda"
  description="What this site collects, what it loads from elsewhere, and how to get a screenshot of your site taken down."
>
  <header>
    <h1 class="page-title">Privacy</h1>
    <p class="standfirst">
      Short version: this site is a pile of static files. It does not know who
      you are and it is not set up to find out.
    </p>
  </header>

  <div class="prose">
    <section>
      <h2 class="prose__head mono">What I collect</h2>
      <p>
        Nothing. There are no accounts, no forms, no comments, no newsletter
        box. There is nothing here for you to fill in, and nowhere for it to go
        if there were.
      </p>
      <p>
        There is no analytics on this site. No Google Analytics, no Plausible,
        no Vercel Analytics, no tracking pixel, no beacon. It sets no cookies
        and writes nothing to local storage. The fonts are bundled into the
        build and served from this domain, so reading a page here does not tell
        a font CDN that you were reading it.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 class="prose__head mono">The one outside request</h2>
      <p>
        The tool pages are the exception and I would rather name it than claim a
        clean sweep. Each tool row shows the favicon of that tool&rsquo;s own
        website, and those come from Google&rsquo;s favicon service instead of
        my server. So loading <a href="/tools">/tools</a> or any tool page makes
        a request to Google with the tool&rsquo;s domain in the URL, and Google
        sees your IP
        address the way any host does when your browser fetches an image. The
        request is sent with no referrer, so it does not carry which page you
        were on. Nothing else on the site loads from anyone else.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 class="prose__head mono">Server logs</h2>
      <p>
        The site is hosted on Vercel, and Vercel keeps its own standard request
        logs, same as any host. I do not read them, I do not analyse them, and I
        have not wired anything to them. They are a side effect of being on the
        internet, not something I set up to learn about you.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 class="prose__head mono">Screenshots of other people&rsquo;s sites</h2>
      <p>
        The <a href="/sites">/sites</a> gallery is screenshots of other
        people&rsquo;s websites. A pipeline takes them automatically when I save
        a link, and
        every one of them gets a page that links out to the site it came from.
        If one of them is yours and you want it gone, open an issue at
        <a href="https://github.com/aayushmanchanda2" rel="noopener">
          github.com/aayushmanchanda2
        </a>
        and I will take it down. You do not have to explain why. That is the only
        contact route on this site.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 class="prose__head mono">Who owns what</h2>
      <p>
        My writing and my notes are mine. The screenshots and anything I quote
        belong to whoever made the thing they are of. If you want to reuse
        something I wrote, go ahead. A link back is plenty.
      </p>
    </section>
  </div>
</Base>

<style>
  .prose {
    margin-top: clamp(3.5rem, 12vh, 6rem);
    max-width: 62ch;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--hairline);
  }

  .prose section {
    padding-block: clamp(1.75rem, 4vw, 2.25rem);
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--hairline);
  }

  .prose__head {
    color: var(--faint);
    font-weight: 500;
    letter-spacing: 0.1em;
    /* the head is the label; the paragraph under it carries the reading size */
    line-height: 1.6;
  }

  .prose p {
    margin-top: 0.85rem;
    font-size: 1rem;
    line-height: 1.7;
    color: var(--muted);
  }

  @media (max-width: 599px) {
    .prose {
      /* narrower column, so the measure is already short enough */
      max-width: none;
    }
  }
</style>
